Solve these 15 unlabeled problems (mix of Patterns 2, 3, 4).
Table refs: 1)`Employee(id, name, salary, department)`,
2)`Orders(order_id, customer_id, amount, status)`,
3)`Students(id, name, class, marks)`.

1. Departments with more than 2 employees.
2. Department(s) with the highest total salary payout.
3. For each department, count of employees earning above 50000 vs at/below.
4. Customer(s) who placed the fewest orders.
5. Classes with average marks above 70.
6. For each customer, percentage of orders that are 'completed'.
7. Department with the lowest average salary.
8. Count of orders per status, for each customer, as separate columns.
9. Classes with more than 5 students scoring above 90.
10. Customer(s) with the highest number of cancelled orders specifically.
11. For each department, employee count and average salary in the same row.
12. Class(es) with the highest number of failing students (<35 marks).
13. Departments where at least 3 employees earn above the department's own average (⚠ this one may not be solvable with only Patterns 2-4 — if you get stuck, note _why_; this previews Pattern 7).
14. For each department, total salary of employees vs total count, in one row.
15. Customer with the single largest individual order amount (not total — careful, this is `MAX(amount)` not `SUM`).



